  "migrateMobaHelp": "In MobaXterm, right-click User sessions and export them to a file, then choose that .mxtsessions file here (its MobaXterm.ini works too). SSH, Telnet and SFTP sessions come in, and FTP sessions as FTPS hosts, their folders as groups. Passwords stay in MobaXterm. VNC sessions come in as remote screens.",
  "@migrateMobaHelp": {"description": "Text shown for HostFileKind.mobaxterm in the CSV host import."},
  "migrateMRemoteNgDesc": "Its connections, folders as groups",
  "@migrateMRemoteNgDesc": {"description": "Line under the name mRemoteNG in the list of apps a person can import hosts from: what is imported (its saved connections, with its folders becoming groups). mRemoteNG is a Windows program that keeps SSH, VNC and Remote Desktop connections."},
  "migrateMRemoteNgHelp": "Choose mRemoteNG's confCons.xml (in its settings folder), or a file made with File, Export to File. SSH, Telnet and VNC connections come in, their folders as groups. Passwords stay in mRemoteNG. A file that is encrypted as a whole cannot be read: turn that off in mRemoteNG first.",
  "@migrateMRemoteNgHelp": {"description": "Help shown before choosing a file in the import from mRemoteNG: which file to choose (confCons.xml, the program's own name for it, or a file made with its File menu's Export to File), which connections are imported, that passwords are not, and that a fully encrypted file cannot be read."},
  "importFoundMRemoteNg": "Looks like an mRemoteNG connections file. Import its connections?",
  "@importFoundMRemoteNg": {"description": "Question shown when a file a person chose to import turns out to be mRemoteNG's connections file: offers to import its connections."},
  "migrateRemminaDesc": "A connection file, one host each",
  "@migrateRemminaDesc": {"description": "Line under the name Remmina in the list of apps a person can import hosts from: what is imported (one of its connection files, each of which holds one host). Remmina is a Linux remote desktop program."},
  "migrateRemminaHelp": "Choose a .remmina file from Remmina's folder (~/.local/share/remmina). SSH, SFTP and VNC connections come in. The password stays in Remmina.",
  "@migrateRemminaHelp": {"description": "Help shown before choosing a file in the import from Remmina: which file to choose (a .remmina file from its data folder, whose path is given as on Linux), which connections are imported, and that the password is not."},
  "importFoundRemmina": "Looks like a Remmina connection file. Import it?",
  "@importFoundRemmina": {"description": "Question shown when a file a person chose to import turns out to be a Remmina connection file: offers to import it."},
  "migrateRdpFileTitle": "Remote Desktop file (.rdp)",
  "@migrateRdpFileTitle": {"description": "Name of one source in the list of files a person can import hosts from, and the name the import uses for it: a Remote Desktop file, the kind of file whose name ends in .rdp, in which Windows' Remote Desktop client and other programs save one connection. Use the name Windows itself gives Remote Desktop in the language. Keep (.rdp) as it is."},
  "migrateRdpFileDesc": "One connection saved by Windows or another remote desktop program",
  "@migrateRdpFileDesc": {"description": "Line under the name of the Remote Desktop file source in the list of files a person can import hosts from: what such a file is (one connection, saved by Windows or by another remote desktop program)."},
  "migrateRdpFileHelp": "Choose a .rdp file. Its computer, port and account come in as one Remote Desktop host. A password saved in the file is not read.",
  "@migrateRdpFileHelp": {"description": "Help shown before choosing a file in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file: which file to choose, what is imported (the computer's address, its port and the account's name, as one host), and that a password saved in the file is not read."},
  "migrateHelpRdpToo": "Remote Desktop (RDP) connections come in too.",
  "@migrateHelpRdpToo": {"description": "One sentence added after the help of the imports from MobaXterm, mRemoteNG and Remmina, in a version of the app that connects with Remote Desktop: those programs' Remote Desktop (RDP) connections are imported as well as the kinds the help has just listed. Use the name Windows gives Remote Desktop in the language, and keep RDP in brackets."},
  "importFoundRdpFile": "Looks like a Remote Desktop file. Import its connection?",
  "@importFoundRdpFile": {"description": "Question shown when a file a person chose to import turns out to be a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file: offers to import the one connection it holds."},
  "rdpFileNoAddress": "This file names no computer to connect to.",
  "@rdpFileNoAddress": {"description": "Error in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file: the file does not say which computer to connect to, or what it says is no address, so nothing can be imported."},
  "rdpFileNotePassword": "The file holds a saved password. It was not read.",
  "@rdpFileNotePassword": {"description": "Note in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file, beside the host found: the file had a password saved in it, and the import did not read it."},
  "rdpFileNoteProgram": "The file opens a single program on the remote computer. This host opens the whole desktop.",
  "@rdpFileNoteProgram": {"description": "Note in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file, beside the host found: the file was made to open a single program on the remote computer, and the imported host will show the remote computer's whole desktop, not that program."},
  "rdpFileNoteNla": "The file turns Network Level Authentication off. This host keeps it on.",
  "@rdpFileNoteNla": {"description": "Note in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file, beside the host found: the file switched off Network Level Authentication (the sign-in that happens before the server shows anything), and the imported host leaves it switched on. Use the name Windows gives Network Level Authentication in the language."},
  "rdpFileNoteGateway": "The file connects through a Remote Desktop Gateway ({gateway}), which this version cannot use. This host connects directly, which may only work inside the computer's own network.",
  "@rdpFileNoteGateway": {"description": "Note in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file, beside the host found: the file says to connect through a Remote Desktop Gateway (a server that stands between the internet and the computers of a network), which this version of the app cannot do, so the imported host connects straight to the computer, and that may work only from inside the computer's own network. {gateway} is the gateway's address as the file has it.", "placeholders": {"gateway": {"type": "String"}}},
  "rdpFileNoteGatewayKept": "The host goes through the Remote Desktop Gateway {gateway}, as the file says, and signs in to it with the computer's account. A key of its own for the gateway can be chosen in the host's settings.",
  "@rdpFileNoteGatewayKept": {"description": "Note in the import of a Remote Desktop (.rdp) file, where this version can use a Remote Desktop Gateway: the imported host goes through the gateway the file names ({gateway}, its address as the file has it), signing in to it with the computer's own account; a key of its own can be chosen in the host's settings.", "placeholders": {"gateway": {"type": "String"}}},
  "migrateCrtHelp": "In SecureCRT, choose Tools > Export Settings and save them as an .xml file, then choose that file here (a session's own .ini file works too). SSH, Telnet and SFTP sessions come in, and SecureFX's FTP and FTPS sessions as FTPS hosts, their folders as groups. Passwords stay in SecureCRT.",
  "@migrateCrtHelp": {"description": "Text shown for HostFileKind.securecrt in the CSV host import."},
