  "histPageHow1": "Commands are saved only while recording is on. It is off until you turn it on, on each device.",
  "@histPageHow1": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 1: recording is off until turned on, per device."},
  "histPageHow2": "A command is saved when you press Enter on a line you typed and the terminal shows that line back. A password prompt does not show what you type, so a password is never saved.",
  "@histPageHow2": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 2: what is saved (a typed line the terminal shows back at Enter) and why a password is never saved."},
  "histPageHow3": "A line changed with the arrow keys, or brought back from the shell history with the Up arrow, is not saved: what ran cannot be known for sure.",
  "@histPageHow3": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 3: a line edited with the arrow keys, or brought back with the Up arrow, is not saved."},
  "histPageHow4": "A command that looks like it holds a password or token (such as -p followed by a password, TOKEN=… or an Authorization header) is not saved, unless {option} is on in this page's menu.",
  "@histPageHow4": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 4: commands that look like they hold a secret are not saved unless the menu option is on. {option} is the name of that menu item (Save secret-bearing commands), as the menu says it.", "placeholders": {"option": {"type": "String"}}},
  "histPageHow5": "Folder, result and run time come from the host's shell integration (OSC 133 and OSC 7, sent by tools such as starship or the shell hooks of iTerm2 and VTE). Where a host's shell sends none, those columns stay empty for its commands.",
  "@histPageHow5": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 5: folder, result and run time come from shell integration (OSC 133, OSC 7); without it those columns are empty."},
  "histPageHow6": "A command's output is never saved. Folder, result and run time stay on this device; with sync on, your commands go to your other devices too, end-to-end encrypted. The newest 2,000 are kept.",
  "@histPageHow6": {"description": "How history works dialog, point 6: output is never saved; the extra details stay on the device; the commands sync end-to-end encrypted; the newest 2,000 are kept."},
  "syncInstantDescPull": "{minutes, plural, one{Sends each change at once, and brings in other devices' changes every minute} other{Sends each change at once, and brings in other devices' changes every {minutes} minutes}}",
  "@syncInstantDescPull": {"description": "Under the Instant sync choice: what it does. {minutes} is how often it looks for other devices' changes (5).", "placeholders": {"minutes": {"type": "int"}}},
  "syncMergeNoteThreeWay": "Each item is merged on its own: a change made on one device is kept, and only where the same item changed on two devices does the later change win.",
  "@syncMergeNoteThreeWay": {"description": "Note under the sync panel: how changes from two devices are merged (each item on its own; a later change wins only where both changed it)."},
  "syncNoteCrossingWrites": "This kind of storage takes any write, so it cannot refuse one that crosses another device's. If two of your devices sync in the same moment, one's changes can be replaced in the storage. That device still has them and sends them again the next time it syncs.",
  "@syncNoteCrossingWrites": {"description": "Note under the sync panel, shown only for WebDAV and Google Drive: these stores cannot refuse a write based on an older version, so two devices syncing at the same moment can replace one another's upload; nothing is lost on the device."},
  "accountExportDescAll": "A .zest file of everything that syncs: hosts and groups, keys with their passwords, snippets, port forwards, known hosts, networks, tags, alerts, command history and settings. With a password it is encrypted; without one it is not.",
  "@accountExportDescAll": {"description": "Under Export (a .zest backup file): what the file holds."},
  "syncCloudDeviceLimitPhone": "ZestSSH Cloud syncs up to 7 devices for one person, and this one would be more. Sign out a device you no longer use (More, Account, Devices), then sync this one again. Nothing on this device was changed or lost.",
